# /etc/sudoers.d/pidvs-xwax-lifecycle
#
# Lets the Node server (running as __BOX_USER__, see pidvs-server.service)
# start and stop xwax@N.service on demand - see
# server/src/xwax-lifecycle.js. Each deck's xwax process no longer
# runs continuously from boot (see xwax@.service's own install
# instructions below: daemon-reload only, deliberately NOT enabled) -
# it only exists for as long as a track is actually loaded (or about
# to be), started the moment a LOAD is requested for an empty deck and
# stopped when passthrough turns on. This is what cuts the sustained
# CPU/heat load of two continuously-running real-time timecode
# decoders whenever the box is powered on but not actually being used -
# see the Phase 1 thermal investigation that found xwax@1/xwax@2
# together pulling ~130% combined CPU around the clock, sustaining a
# soft thermal throttle, on hardware sitting completely idle.
#
# Scoped tightly to exactly these two command forms - start or stop a
# specific xwax@N instance - not general systemctl access. The
# absolute path (/usr/bin/systemctl) has to match exactly what
# xwax-lifecycle.js actually invokes (see that file's own comment on
# why it uses an absolute path rather than relying on PATH) - if
# `which systemctl` on the real box ever returns something different,
# both this file and xwax-lifecycle.js's SYSTEMCTL_PATH need updating
# together.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp pidvs-sudoers-xwax-lifecycle /etc/sudoers.d/pidvs-xwax-lifecycle
#   sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/pidvs-xwax-lifecycle
#   sudo visudo -c   # validate syntax before trusting it - a broken
#                     # sudoers.d file can break sudo system-wide

__BOX_USER__ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start xwax@*.service, /usr/bin/systemctl stop xwax@*.service
